::[A fault reaches the bearer]

## Testing the row

The tests read one loom the way a build does: they parse it and run the row over the tree. `built` gives the document, `bracesOf` gives the braces of its first heading, and `statedBy` lists what those braces state, each key with its value. `textAt` reads the text a position covers, so a test can check that a parameter points at the author's own words.

`anchorIn` gives the first anchor of a section's code, and `boxIn` the box a line opens. `greeting` builds an anchor with braces from its pieces, so the code of this chapter holds no anchor of its own. `messagesIn` gathers the messages of the faults that stand in a pair of braces: on its parameters, on a label beside them, or on its label alone. `fixesIn` gathers the fixes those faults offer.

=>

import { describe, expect, it } from 'bun:test'
import { Effect, Match, pipe, Schema } from 'effect'
import type { Fix, Health, Position } from '@athrio/loom-ast/LoomNode'
import { LoomDocumentSchema, type LoomDocument } from '@athrio/loom-ast/LoomAst'
import {
  type Keys,
  type ParametersToken,
  sectionKinds,
  type SpecifierToken,
} from '@athrio/loom-ast/LoomTokens'
import {
  checkedDocument,
  claimedDocument,
  coreKeys,
  expandedDocument,
  LoomDocumentBuilder,
  type SectionKind,
} from '#ast/LoomDocumentBuilder'
import { parseDocument, parsedDocument, ParseLayer } from './parse'

const built = (text: string): LoomDocument =>
  Effect.runSync(parseDocument(text).pipe(Effect.provide(ParseLayer)))

const bracesOf = (heading: string): ParametersToken | undefined =>
  built(`${heading}\n\nProse.\n`).sections[0]?.heading.specifier

const statedBy = (heading: string): ReadonlyArray<readonly [string, string | undefined]> =>
  (bracesOf(heading)?.parameters ?? []).map(
    (parameter) => [parameter.key.value, parameter.values[0]?.value] as const,
  )

const textAt = (text: string, position: Position): string =>
  text.slice(position.start.offset, position.end.offset)

const greeting = (braces: string): string => `::${'['}Greeting]${braces}`

const anchorIn = (line: string) =>
  built(`# A\n\n=>\n\n${line}\n`).sections[0]?.code.flatMap((weft) => weft.anchors)[0]

const boxIn = (line: string) =>
  built(`# A\n\n${line}\nBody.\n:::\n`).sections[0]?.preamble.flatMap((weft) =>
    weft.box ? [weft.box] : [],
  )[0]

const judgedIn = (
  braces: SpecifierToken | ParametersToken | undefined,
): ReadonlyArray<{ readonly health: Health }> =>
  Match.value(braces).pipe(
    Match.when(Match.undefined, () => []),
    Match.when({ type: 'Parameters' }, (held) => [...held.parameters, ...held.labels]),
    Match.when({ type: 'Specifier' }, (labelled) => [labelled.label]),
    Match.exhaustive,
  )

const messagesIn = (braces: SpecifierToken | ParametersToken | undefined): ReadonlyArray<string> =>
  judgedIn(braces).flatMap((judged) => judged.health.diagnostics.map((found) => found.message))

const fixesIn = (braces: SpecifierToken | ParametersToken | undefined): ReadonlyArray<Fix> =>
  judgedIn(braces).flatMap((judged) =>
    judged.health.diagnostics.flatMap((found) => found.fixes ?? []),
  )

## A label becomes a parameter

A label that names a language becomes a `lang` parameter, and a label that names a kind becomes a `kind` parameter, in any case of letters. A language named by two words stays one parameter. Parameters the author wrote in full stay as they are.

=>

describe("expanding a heading's label", () => {
  it('writes a language in full', () => {
    expect(statedBy('# Build {Bash}')).toEqual([['lang', 'Bash']])
  })

  it('writes a kind in full, in any case of letters', () => {
    expect(statedBy('# Install {Tangle}')).toEqual([['kind', 'Tangle']])
    expect(statedBy('# Install {tangle}')).toEqual([['kind', 'tangle']])
  })

  it('keeps a language of two words whole', () => {
    expect(statedBy('# Build {Emacs Lisp}')).toEqual([['lang', 'Emacs Lisp']])
  })

  it('leaves parameters as the author wrote them', () => {
    expect(statedBy('# Install {kind=Tangle lang=sh}')).toEqual([
      ['kind', 'Tangle'],
      ['lang', 'sh'],
    ])
  })
})

## A written parameter points at the author's words

The parameter the pass writes covers the label in the loom. Its key covers nothing, and stands where the label starts.

=>

describe('a parameter the pass writes', () => {
  const text = '# Install {Tangle}\n\nProse.\n'
  const [kind] = built(text).sections[0]?.heading.specifier?.parameters ?? []

  it('covers the label the author wrote', () => {
    expect(kind && textAt(text, kind.position)).toBe('Tangle')
  })

  it('has a key with no width, where the label starts', () => {
    expect(kind?.key.source).toBe('')
    expect(kind?.key.position.start.offset).toBe(kind?.position.start.offset)
    expect(kind?.key.position.end.offset).toBe(kind?.position.start.offset)
  })
})

## A label nobody could read keeps its fault

Empty braces hold a label the tokeniser faults, since it cannot read one. The pass gives such braces no parameters, and the fault stays on the braces.

=>

describe('a label the tokeniser could not read', () => {
  it('states nothing and keeps its fault', () => {
    const braces = bracesOf('# Build {}')
    expect(braces?.parameters).toEqual([])
    expect(braces?.health.diagnostics.map((found) => found.message)).toEqual([
      'These braces are empty. Write a label in them, or remove them.',
    ])
  })
})

## A label beside parameters is a fault

A label written beside parameters is a fault on the label, on any bearer of braces. The fault's fix writes the label in full under the key it stands for: `lang` or `kind` on a heading, and `kind` on an anchor. The fault fails the braces and their bearer.

=>

describe('a label beside parameters', () => {
  it('is written in full under the key it stands for, on a heading', () => {
    expect(messagesIn(bracesOf('# Build {Bash kind=Tangle}'))).toEqual([
      'A label stands alone only in braces that hold nothing else. Write it in full here, as `lang="Bash"`.',
    ])
    expect(messagesIn(bracesOf('# Build {Tangle lang=sh}'))).toEqual([
      'A label stands alone only in braces that hold nothing else. Write it in full here, as `kind="Tangle"`.',
    ])
  })

  it('offers the full form as its fix, and fails the anchor that bears it', () => {
    const anchor = anchorIn(greeting('{To be settled provides=greet}'))
    const [label] = anchor?.specifier?.type === 'Parameters' ? anchor.specifier.labels : []
    expect(label?.health.diagnostics[0]?.fixes?.map((fix) => fix.text)).toEqual([
      'kind="To be settled"',
    ])
    expect(anchor?.specifier?.health.status).toBe('error')
    expect(anchor?.health.status).toBe('error')
  })
})

## A section carries the kind it claimed

A section claims a kind by a label or by a parameter, in any case of letters, and carries the core's name for it. A heading that names a language alone, or nothing, claims no kind.

=>

describe('claiming a kind', () => {
  const kindOf = (heading: string): string | undefined =>
    built(`${heading}\n\nProse.\n`).sections[0]?.kind

  it('claims the kind a label names', () => {
    expect(kindOf('# Install {Tangle}')).toBe('Tangle')
    expect(kindOf('# An idea {concept}')).toBe('Concept')
  })

  it('claims the kind a parameter names', () => {
    expect(kindOf('# Install {kind=tangle lang=sh}')).toBe('Tangle')
  })

  it('claims nothing for a language, or for no braces', () => {
    expect(kindOf('# Build {Bash}')).toBeUndefined()
    expect(kindOf('# Plain')).toBeUndefined()
  })
})

## A kind nothing declares is a fault

A kind the core does not know claims nothing. Its value carries the fault, and the braces and the heading carry its status.

=>

describe('a kind nothing declares', () => {
  const heading = built('# Build {kind=Bogus}\n\nProse.\n').sections[0]?.heading

  it('claims nothing', () => {
    expect(built('# Build {kind=Bogus}\n\nProse.\n').sections[0]?.kind).toBeUndefined()
  })

  it('is a fault on the value that names it', () => {
    expect(messagesIn(heading?.specifier)).toEqual([
      'A heading takes no `Bogus` kind. It takes `Tangle`, `Concept`, and `Manifest`.',
    ])
    expect(heading?.specifier?.health.status).toBe('error')
    expect(heading?.health.status).toBe('error')
  })
})

## A key no bearer takes, and a key written twice

Each bearer of braces takes its own keys. A key it does not take is a fault on that key, and so is a key written a second time. The fault fails the braces and their bearer. A repeated key carries the fault for the repeat alone.

=>

describe('checking the keys of braces', () => {
  it("faults a key a heading does not take, and fails the heading's braces", () => {
    const heading = built('# Build {flavour=dry}\n\nProse.\n').sections[0]?.heading
    expect(messagesIn(heading?.specifier)).toEqual([
      'A heading takes no `flavour` key. It takes `lang` and `kind`.',
    ])
    expect(heading?.specifier?.health.status).toBe('error')
    expect(heading?.health.status).toBe('error')
  })

  it('faults a key written twice', () => {
    expect(messagesIn(bracesOf('# Build {lang=Bash lang=sh}'))).toEqual([
      '`lang` is written twice in these braces. Keep one.',
    ])
  })

  it("checks an anchor's keys, and fails the anchor", () => {
    const anchor = anchorIn(greeting('{provides=a colour=red}'))
    expect(messagesIn(anchor?.specifier)).toEqual([
      'An anchor takes no `colour` key. It takes `kind` and `provides`.',
    ])
    expect(anchor?.health.status).toBe('error')
    expect(messagesIn(anchorIn(greeting('{provides=a provides=b}'))?.specifier)).toEqual([
      '`provides` is written twice in these braces. Keep one.',
    ])
  })

  it("checks a box's keys", () => {
    expect(messagesIn(boxIn('::: Cells {kind=Note width=480}')?.specifier)).toEqual([
      'A box takes no `width` key. It takes `kind`, `provides`, and `anchors`.',
    ])
  })

  it('gives a repeated key the fault for the repeat alone', () => {
    expect(messagesIn(anchorIn(greeting('{kind=Module kind=Bogus}'))?.specifier)).toEqual([
      '`kind` is written twice in these braces. Keep one.',
    ])
  })

  it('leaves sound braces as they were', () => {
    const text = '# Build {kind=Tangle lang=sh}\n\nProse.\n'
    const parsed = Effect.runSync(parsedDocument(text).pipe(Effect.provide(ParseLayer)))
    expect<unknown>(built(text).sections[0]?.heading.specifier).toEqual(
      parsed.sections[0]?.heading.specifier,
    )
  })
})

## A value is read through its key's type

A key's type decides how its values read. The core's own keys hold text, so the tests hand the pass a table in which a heading takes keys of every other type as well, the way a trope's keys reach the pass. `typed` runs the row over one heading with that table.

A value that reads raises nothing. A value that does not read carries a fault that says what the type wanted, and the fault stands on that value, so one bad value in a list is marked where it stands. A key that holds one value faults a list, and a key that holds a list takes one value alone.

=>

describe('checking the values of braces', () => {
  const keys: typeof coreKeys = {
    ...coreKeys,
    heading: {
      ...coreKeys.heading,
      width: 'number',
      draft: 'boolean',
      status: { choice: ['draft', 'done'] },
      locale: 'locale',
      related: { list: 'reference' },
      sizes: { list: 'number' },
    } satisfies Keys,
  }

  const typed = (braces: string) => {
    const text = `# Plan ${braces}\n\nProse.\n`
    const parsed = Effect.runSync(parsedDocument(text).pipe(Effect.provide(ParseLayer)))
    const checked = pipe(
      parsed,
      expandedDocument(sectionKinds),
      claimedDocument(sectionKinds),
      checkedDocument(keys),
    )
    return { text, heading: checked.sections[0]?.heading }
  }

  const saidOf = (braces: string) => messagesIn(typed(braces).heading?.specifier)

  it('reads a value of each type without a fault', () => {
    expect(
      saidOf('{width=480 draft=true status=done locale=pt-BR related=[Anchor, Sink] sizes=[1, 2.5]}'),
    ).toEqual([])
  })

  it('faults a value that is no number', () => {
    expect(saidOf('{width=wide}')).toEqual([
      '`width` takes a number, and `wide` is not one. Write a number, such as `480`.',
    ])
  })

  it('faults a word that a boolean or a choice does not take', () => {
    expect(saidOf('{draft=yes}')).toEqual(['`draft` takes no `yes`. It takes `true` and `false`.'])
    expect(saidOf('{status=maybe}')).toEqual([
      '`status` takes no `maybe`. It takes `draft` and `done`.',
    ])
  })

  it('faults a value that is no language tag', () => {
    expect(saidOf('{locale=pt_BR}')).toEqual([
      '`locale` takes a language tag, and `pt_BR` is not one. Write a tag such as `et` or `pt-BR`.',
    ])
  })

  it('marks the one value of a list that does not read, and fails the heading', () => {
    const { text, heading } = typed('{sizes=[1, two]}')
    const [found] = (heading?.specifier?.parameters ?? []).flatMap(
      (parameter) => parameter.health.diagnostics,
    )
    expect(found && textAt(text, found.position)).toBe('two')
    expect(heading?.health.status).toBe('error')
  })

  it('faults a list under a key that holds one value', () => {
    expect(messagesIn(bracesOf('# Build {lang=[Bash, sh]}'))).toEqual([
      '`lang` takes one value, and these braces give it several. Keep one.',
    ])
  })

  it('lets a key that holds a list hold one value', () => {
    expect(messagesIn(anchorIn(greeting('{provides=greet}'))?.specifier)).toEqual([])
  })
})

## A kind of section a trope declares

A trope's kinds of section reach the row as `declared`. The tests hand it one kind, `Option`, whose headings take a `status` that is one of two words, and `builtWith` runs the row over one heading with that kind declared.

A label that names the declared kind states a kind, where the same label states a language when nothing is declared. A heading of the kind takes the kind's parameters, each read through its type, and no heading of another kind takes them. A kind that nothing declares lists the declared kinds after the core's.

=>

describe('a kind of section a trope declares', () => {
  const declared: ReadonlyArray<SectionKind> = [
    { kind: 'Option', parameters: { status: { choice: ['chosen', 'rejected'] } } },
  ]

  const builtWith = (heading: string, kinds: ReadonlyArray<SectionKind> = declared) =>
    Effect.runSync(
      Effect.gen(function* () {
        const documents = yield* LoomDocumentBuilder
        return documents.build(yield* parsedDocument(`${heading}\n\nProse.\n`), kinds)
      }).pipe(Effect.provide(ParseLayer)),
    ).sections[0]

  const stated = (heading: string, kinds?: ReadonlyArray<SectionKind>) =>
    (builtWith(heading, kinds)?.heading.specifier?.parameters ?? []).map(
      (parameter) => [parameter.key.value, parameter.values[0]?.value] as const,
    )

  const said = (heading: string) => messagesIn(builtWith(heading)?.heading.specifier)

  it('is claimed by a label, which states a language when nothing declares the kind', () => {
    expect(stated('## Postgres {Option}')).toEqual([['kind', 'Option']])
    expect(builtWith('## Postgres {option}')?.kind).toBe('Option')
    expect(stated('## Postgres {Option}', [])).toEqual([['lang', 'Option']])
  })

  it("takes the kind's parameters, read through their types", () => {
    expect(said('## Postgres {kind=Option status=chosen}')).toEqual([])
    expect(said('## Postgres {kind=Option status=maybe}')).toEqual([
      '`status` takes no `maybe`. It takes `chosen` and `rejected`.',
    ])
  })

  it('lists the parameters of the kind among the keys its heading takes', () => {
    expect(said('## Postgres {kind=Option flavour=dry}')).toEqual([
      'A heading takes no `flavour` key. It takes `lang`, `kind`, and `status`.',
    ])
  })

  it("keeps the kind's parameters from a heading of another kind", () => {
    expect(said('## Postgres {status=chosen}')).toEqual([
      'A heading takes no `status` key. It takes `lang` and `kind`.',
    ])
  })

  it('lists the declared kinds after the core kinds, for a kind nothing declares', () => {
    expect(said('## Postgres {kind=Bogus}')).toEqual([
      'A heading takes no `Bogus` kind. It takes `Tangle`, `Concept`, `Manifest`, and `Option`.',
    ])
  })
})

## An anchor's kind is one of its own

An anchor's kind is `To be settled` or `Module`, in any case of letters. Any other kind is a fault on the value that names it, written as a label or as a parameter, and it fails the anchor. A kind written by its former name gets a fault of its own, whose fix writes the present name over the former one. A box's kind is left for the book to judge.

=>

describe("the kind an anchor's braces name", () => {
  it('faults a label that names no kind of anchor, and fails the anchor', () => {
    const anchor = anchorIn(greeting('{x}'))
    expect(messagesIn(anchor?.specifier)).toEqual([
      'An anchor takes no `x` kind. It takes `To be settled` and `Module`.',
    ])
    expect(anchor?.health.status).toBe('error')
  })

  it('faults a parameter that names no kind of anchor', () => {
    expect(messagesIn(anchorIn(greeting('{kind=Term}'))?.specifier)).toEqual([
      'An anchor takes no `Term` kind. It takes `To be settled` and `Module`.',
    ])
  })

  it('reads the kind in any case of letters', () => {
    const anchor = anchorIn(greeting('{to be settled}'))
    expect(messagesIn(anchor?.specifier)).toEqual([])
    expect(anchor?.health.status).toBe('ok')
  })

  it('tells the author the present name of a kind written by its former name', () => {
    const anchor = anchorIn(greeting('{Reference}'))
    expect(messagesIn(anchor?.specifier)).toEqual([
      'The `Reference` kind is now called `Module`. Write `Module`.',
    ])
    expect(fixesIn(anchor?.specifier)).toEqual([{ title: 'Write `Module`', text: 'Module' }])
    expect(anchor?.health.status).toBe('error')
  })

  it('knows a former name written as a parameter, in any case of letters', () => {
    expect(messagesIn(anchorIn(greeting('{kind=reference provides=greet}'))?.specifier)).toEqual([
      'The `reference` kind is now called `Module`. Write `Module`.',
    ])
  })

  it("leaves a box's kind for the book to judge", () => {
    expect(messagesIn(boxIn('::: Cells {Mermaid}')?.specifier)).toEqual([])
  })
})

## The row writes the tree it claims

The tree the row returns decodes against the schema of the `LoomDocument`. The tree the parser built does not, while a heading in it still holds a label. So the test fails for a pass that leaves a label behind.

=>

describe('the tree the row writes', () => {
  const text = '# Build {Bash}\n\nProse.\n\n# Install {kind=Tangle lang=sh}\n\nProse.\n'
  const decoded = Schema.decodeUnknownSync(LoomDocumentSchema)

  it('decodes as a LoomDocument', () => {
    expect(() => decoded(built(text))).not.toThrow()
  })

  it('differs from the tree the parser built', () => {
    const parsed = Effect.runSync(parsedDocument(text).pipe(Effect.provide(ParseLayer)))
    expect(() => decoded(parsed)).toThrow()
  })
})

